# Normalize line endings.
#
# Without this, git stores whatever bytes the working tree happens to hold.
# On Windows, editors and tooling (PowerShell redirects, Python's
# pathlib.write_text, some IDEs) silently rewrite a file to CRLF, and the
# result is a "phantom diff": every line shows as changed when nothing did.
# That churn buries real changes in review — a two-line edit has appeared as
# 1200+ modified lines.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` makes git normalize to LF in the object database on
# commit and check out LF, regardless of each machine's core.autocrlf. It
# travels with the repo, so CI and every contributor get it too. Binary files
# are detected automatically and left untouched.
* text=auto eol=lf
# Belt and braces for files that MUST be LF: a shell script or Dockerfile with
# CRLF fails on Linux with `bad interpreter: \r: No such file or directory`.
*.sh    text eol=lf
*.bash  text eol=lf
*.yml   text eol=lf
*.yaml  text eol=lf
Dockerfile      text eol=lf
Dockerfile.*    text eol=lf
docker-compose.* text eol=lf
# Never touch these — declaring them keeps `text=auto` heuristics out of it.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.pdf   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary
*.otf   binary
*.zip filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
*.gz    binary
